        "body": "Re the \"one decision\" in the description: I'd keep the routing, but say so on stderr rather than doing it silently.\n\nNobody reaches these keys through `glab config set` by accident: glab itself updates them via `cfg.Set`, not this command. So whoever runs `glab config set duo_cli_binary_version ...` typed it deliberately (usually debugging or forcing a re-download) and asked for the local file. Silently writing global instead changes every repository without telling them. A one-line notice keeps the convenience and removes the surprise:\n\n```suggestion:-3+0\n\t// A key glab maintains for itself is read from the global file only, so a\n\t// local write would land where nothing reads it and the old local value\n\t// would keep winning. Route those to the global file instead.\n\trouted := !o.isGlobal && o.hostname == \"\" && config.IsGlobalOnlyKey(o.key)\n\twriteGlobal := o.isGlobal || o.hostname != \"\" || routed\n\tif routed {\n\t\to.io.LogErrorf(\"%s %s is managed by glab and is always stored in the global configuration: %s\\n\",\n\t\t\to.io.Color().DotWarnIcon(), o.key, config.ConfigFile())\n\t}\n```\n\n`TestConfigSet_GlobalOnlyKeyWritesGloballyWithoutTheFlag` would then need to assert the notice instead of `assert.Empty(t, out.Stderr())`, e.g. `assert.Contains(t, out.Stderr(), \"always stored in the global configuration\")`."
      },
